#!/usr/bin/env bash
# ralph — fresh-context implementation loop against a checklist.
#
# Unlike continuity-based loops (e.g. the Ralph Wiggum plugin), every
# attempt is a genuinely fresh `claude -p` call with zero conversational
# memory. Since nothing carries over, ALL state that matters must live on
# disk — the PLAN.md checklist, git history, and test results are the only
# memory a fresh attempt has.
#
# Two files, two owners:
#   SPEC.md  design reference, written once by /ralph-spec. Nothing —
#            not ralph, not a claude -p attempt, not a [mvn] item — ever
#            edits it again. ralph's own bash never even opens it.
#   PLAN.md  the checklist ralph actually reads and mutates. Pure run
#            state (which items are done), safe for either a claude -p
#            session or ralph's own bash to check off, depending on the
#            item's type below.
#
# Item types in PLAN.md:
#   normal item   `- [ ] <task> ... <verify command>`
#                 A fresh `claude -p` session implements it, tests it,
#                 clears --review-gate, checks it off in PLAN.md, and
#                 commits. ralph's own bash never edits PLAN.md for these.
#   [mvn] item    `- [ ] [mvn] <description> :: goal=<groupId:artifactId:version:goal>
#                 [prop=<key>=<value>]... [pom=<path>] then=<verify command>`
#                 ralph assembles and runs the `mvn` invocation itself from
#                 those fields — NO claude -p call, NO --review-gate. On
#                 success, ralph itself flips that exact line's checkbox and
#                 commits — a real behavior change from the "delegate all
#                 mutation to the LLM" model above, scoped only to [mvn]
#                 items in PLAN.md (SPEC.md is never touched by either
#                 item type). On failure, the working tree is left exactly
#                 as the run left it — no auto-discard — since Maven plugin
#                 goals are expected to be idempotent and a human reviewing
#                 an exhausted run needs real evidence, not a clean slate.
#                 Intended for deterministic, plugin-shaped work (an
#                 OpenRewrite recipe, a Spotless/Checkstyle pass, a
#                 versions-maven-plugin bump) — never bespoke logic. Maven
#                 only; Gradle's equivalent conventions are out of scope.
#
# Control flow: for each checklist item, retry it up to --max-attempts
# times, checking success by exit code ([mvn] items) or by whether the
# item got checked off (normal items). Succeed -> advance to the next
# item. Exhaust attempts without success -> stop the whole run for human
# review; a task that fresh-context retries (or a deterministic command)
# can't crack needs a person, not another retry.
#
# Usage: ralph [--plan PLAN.md] [--max-attempts N] [--items N] [--review-gate full|light|none] [--model MODEL]
#   --max-attempts N   retries allowed on a SINGLE item before giving up on it (default 3)
#   --items N          how many items to complete this run before stopping and
#                       returning control (default: unlimited — run to completion).
#                       Use --items 1 to pair on one item at a time: review what
#                       happened, then re-invoke for the next.
#   --review-gate MODE what a normal (non-[mvn]) attempt must clear, beyond tests,
#                       before an item can be checked off (default: full). No-op
#                       for [mvn] items — nothing to gate, no LLM runs.
#                         full  - /code-review at high effort: broader
#                                 coverage, may surface uncertain findings.
#                                 Any surviving finding blocks.
#                         light - /code-review at medium effort: fewer,
#                                 higher-confidence findings. Cheaper.
#                         none  - tests-only gate (no /code-review).
#                       Never uses /code-review's `ultra` level — that one is
#                       user-triggered and billed, not launchable from here.
#
# Relies on --permission-mode auto being valid in your environment (verified
# via `claude --help` at write time) — headless -p calls can't answer
# interactive prompts, so this is passed explicitly rather than assumed.

set -euo pipefail

usage() {
  cat <<'EOF'
ralph — fresh-context implementation loop against a checklist

Every attempt is a genuinely fresh `claude -p` call with zero conversational
memory. All state that matters lives on disk: the PLAN.md checklist, git
history, and test results. For each checklist item, ralph retries it (fresh
context each time) until it passes, then advances to the next item.

SPEC.md (design reference, written once by /ralph-spec) and PLAN.md (the
checklist ralph reads/mutates) are separate files — ralph never opens
SPEC.md itself.

Usage: ralph [options]

Options:
  --plan FILE             Checklist to work from (default: PLAN.md)
  --max-attempts N        Retries allowed on a SINGLE item before giving up
                          on it and stopping for human review (default: 3)
  --items N               How many items to complete this run before
                          stopping and returning control (default:
                          unlimited — run to completion). Use --items 1 to
                          pair on one item at a time.
  --review-gate MODE      What a normal (non-[mvn]) attempt must clear,
                          beyond tests, before an item can be checked off
                          (default: full). No-op for [mvn] items.
                            full   /code-review at high effort: broader
                                   coverage, may surface uncertain
                                   findings. Any surviving finding blocks.
                            light  /code-review at medium effort: fewer,
                                   higher-confidence findings.
                            none   tests-only gate, no /code-review.
                          Never uses /code-review's `ultra` level — that is
                          user-triggered and billed, not launchable here.
  --model MODEL           Model to use for each attempt (default: unset,
                          inherits claude's own default)
  -h, --help              Show this help and exit

Requires a git repo (run /onboard or git init first) — git history is the
only memory a fresh attempt has of what came before it.

Item types (see PLAN.md itself, or the header comment in this script, for
the full [mvn] field syntax):
  normal item   claude -p implements/tests/reviews/checks off/commits.
  [mvn] item    `- [ ] [mvn] <desc> :: goal=... [prop=...]... [pom=...] then=...`
                ralph assembles and runs `mvn` directly, no LLM call. On
                success ralph itself checks it off and commits; on failure
                the tree is left dirty for inspection.

Progress: .ralph/run.log (one line per attempt), .ralph/logs/item-NNN-attempt-N.log
(full transcript per attempt).
EOF
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OKF concept docs (SPEC.md §11)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# SPEC.md §11 has each attempt's prompt carry the concept docs for the files its
# item is about. This is the resolution half of that, and only that: a checklist
# item's text goes in, the paths of the concept files it names come out, one per
# line. Nothing here opens a concept, and nothing here builds a prompt — the
# caller that pastes these into PROMPT is the next item in PLAN.md, which is why
# ralph's own run below does not call this yet.
#
# The co-location rule is SPEC.md §5's and is spelled out again here rather than
# asked of `okf`: SPEC.md §7 has no subcommand that answers "which concept
# documents this file", and ralph has to keep working on a machine where okf is
# not installed at all. So okf's presence is read as a yes/no — is this repo
# being run with the OKF half of the toolkit — and the derivation itself is
# these few lines of string handling. bin/okf's has_concept is the same rule
# written for the same reasons; a change to one belongs in the other.

# SPEC.md §4's reserved OKF filenames. Neither is ever a source's concept:
# `index.md` is the directory's own document and `log.md` is left to OKF's own
# semantics. A source whose stem lands on one — `index.ts`, `log.py` — has no
# concept to send, and sending the directory's index instead would describe the
# wrong thing entirely.
RALPH_RESERVED_CONCEPTS=(index.md log.md)

# Whether the okf half of the toolkit is installed. ralph runs perfectly well
# without it — every lookup below is simply skipped and nothing is printed — so
# this is a question, never an error.
have_okf() { command -v okf > /dev/null 2>&1; }

# One whitespace-delimited token with the decoration a sentence wraps a path in
# taken off both ends: `bin/ralph`, (tests/toolkit.sh), **install.sh**, and
# "see commands/onboard.md." all leave the same path behind. Membership is
# tested against a set of characters rather than a glob so that the set can hold
# every bracket, quote and backtick without any of them needing to be escaped
# into a pattern.
strip_token_decoration() { # $1 = a raw token
  local token="$1"
  local lead="\`'\"([{<*" trail="\`'\")]}>*,;:.!?"
  while [ -n "$token" ] && [[ $lead == *"${token:0:1}"* ]]; do
    token="${token#?}"
  done
  while [ -n "$token" ] && [[ $trail == *"${token: -1}"* ]]; do
    token="${token%?}"
  done
  printf '%s' "$token"
}

# Whether a stripped token could be a path in this repository at all: made of
# the characters a repository path is made of, and carrying either a directory
# separator or a dot. A pre-filter and not the decision — what settles it is
# whether the file is really there — so it is drawn wide enough for `bin/ralph`,
# `install.sh` and `.editorconfig` alike, and left narrow enough to turn away a
# URL (whose `:` is not in the set) and ordinary prose. It lets through plenty
# that is not a path — `code.content_hash` is shaped exactly like one — and that
# is the pre-filter working as intended rather than a hole in it.
#
# A token holding `..` is refused outright: an item's text is not a trusted path
# and nothing about resolving one should be able to walk out of the repository.
looks_like_path() { # $1 = a stripped token
  local token="$1"
  [ -n "$token" ] || return 1
  [[ "$token" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._/+@-]+$ ]] || return 1
  if [[ "$token" == *..* ]]; then
    return 1
  fi
  [[ "$token" == */* || "$token" == *.* ]]
}

# Whether a file carries a SPEC.md §4 frontmatter block: `---` on line 1, closed
# by the next line that is exactly `---`. Both ends, because a file that opens a
# block and stops is a `/okf-generate` interrupted part way through, and a
# README sitting beside a source of the same name is prose. Pasting either into
# an attempt's prompt as documentation is worse than sending nothing.
has_concept_frontmatter() { # $1 = path to a file
  local line opened=0
  # `|| [ -n "$line" ]` so a last line with no newline after it is still read.
  while IFS= read -r line || [ -n "$line" ]; do
    # A CRLF checkout, or an editor's UTF-8 BOM, does not change what the line
    # says and must not make a concept unrecognisable.
    line="${line#$'\xef\xbb\xbf'}"
    line="${line%$'\r'}"
    if [ "$opened" -eq 0 ]; then
      [ "$line" = "---" ] || return 1
      opened=1
      continue
    fi
    [ "$line" != "---" ] || return 0
  # stderr redirected before the input, so the shell's own complaint about an
  # unreadable file is caught rather than printed into ralph's output.
  done 2> /dev/null < "$1"
  return 1
}

# The concept file co-located with one source, printed when there is one.
# Returns 1, having printed nothing, when there is not — which is the ordinary
# answer for most of the tokens a checklist item contains.
#
# SPEC.md §5's rule: `UserException.java` -> `UserException.md` in the same
# directory. The stem is the name minus its final extension, so `helper.ts`
# gives `helper.md` and not `helper.ts.md`; a name that is all extension — a
# dotfile such as `.gitignore` — keeps its whole name, since stripping it would
# leave nothing and every dotfile in a directory would share the one concept.
#
# The source itself has to exist. That is more than "the concept exists", and
# deliberately: `code.content_hash` and `generated.by` are shaped exactly like
# paths, and without this a repo that happened to hold a `code.md` would have
# ralph paste it in as documentation of a field name.
concept_for_source() { # $1 = a repo-relative source path
  local path="$1" dir base stem concept reserved
  [ -f "$path" ] || return 1

  base="${path##*/}"
  case "$path" in
    */*) dir="${path%/*}/" ;;
    *) dir="" ;;
  esac
  case "$base" in
    ?*.*) stem="${base%.*}" ;;
    *) stem="$base" ;;
  esac
  concept="$dir$stem.md"

  [ -f "$concept" ] || return 1
  # A markdown source is not its own concept. `-ef` rather than a string
  # compare, because on a case-insensitive filesystem `Notes.md` and `notes.md`
  # are one file that two strings cannot tell apart.
  [ ! "$concept" -ef "$path" ] || return 1
  has_concept_frontmatter "$concept" || return 1
  for reserved in "${RALPH_RESERVED_CONCEPTS[@]}"; do
    [ ! "$concept" -ef "$dir$reserved" ] || return 1
  done

  printf '%s\n' "$concept"
}

# The concept docs for the files a checklist item names, one repo-relative path
# per line, in the order the item names them and each printed at most once.
#
# Prints nothing at all — and still succeeds — when okf is not installed, when
# the item names no path, or when nothing it names has a concept beside it. A
# checklist item is prose written for a person, so most of what it holds is not
# a path and most of the paths it does hold have no concept yet; producing
# nothing is the common case and is not a failure.
item_concept_docs() { # $1 = a checklist item's text
  have_okf || return 0

  local text="$1" token concept prev seen
  local -a tokens=() emitted=()
  # `read -a` rather than letting word splitting do it: an item's text really
  # does contain globs — PLAN.md says `commands/*.md` — and an unquoted
  # expansion would match those against the working directory before this
  # function ever saw them.
  IFS=$' \t' read -r -a tokens <<< "$text"

  for token in ${tokens[@]+"${tokens[@]}"}; do
    token="$(strip_token_decoration "$token")"
    token="${token#./}"
    # SPEC.md §4 writes paths bundle-absolute, with a leading `/`. Taking it off
    # resolves the path against the repo rather than against the filesystem
    # root, which is also what keeps `/code-review` and the other slash commands
    # in an item's text from being looked up in `/`.
    token="${token#/}"
    looks_like_path "$token" || continue
    concept="$(concept_for_source "$token")" || continue

    seen=false
    for prev in ${emitted[@]+"${emitted[@]}"}; do
      if [ "$prev" = "$concept" ]; then
        seen=true
        break
      fi
    done
    if [ "$seen" = true ]; then
      continue
    fi
    emitted+=("$concept")
    printf '%s\n' "$concept"
  done
  return 0
}

# Sourced rather than executed — which is how tests/toolkit.sh calls the helpers
# above directly, the same way it sources bin/okf — stop here. Everything below
# this line is one run of the loop: it reads a command line, writes into
# .ralph/, and spawns `claude`. Executed, whether from the checkout or from
# $PATH, $0 and BASH_SOURCE[0] are the one path and the run proceeds as always.
if [ "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" != "$0" ]; then
  return 0
fi

PLAN="PLAN.md"
MAX_ATTEMPTS=3
ITEMS_LIMIT=0   # 0 = unlimited
MODEL=""
REVIEW_GATE="full"

while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
  case "$1" in
    -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
    --plan) PLAN="$2"; shift 2 ;;
    --max-attempts) MAX_ATTEMPTS="$2"; shift 2 ;;
    --items) ITEMS_LIMIT="$2"; shift 2 ;;
    --review-gate) REVIEW_GATE="$2"; shift 2 ;;
    --model) MODEL="$2"; shift 2 ;;
    *) echo "ralph: unknown flag: $1" >&2; usage >&2; exit 1 ;;
  esac
done

case "$REVIEW_GATE" in
  full|light|none) ;;
  *) echo "ralph: --review-gate must be full, light, or none (got: $REVIEW_GATE)" >&2; exit 1 ;;
esac

if [[ ! -f "$PLAN" ]]; then
  echo "ralph: $PLAN not found. Write a checklist (one '- [ ] task' per item) at $PLAN — typically generated by /ralph-spec alongside SPEC.md. See 'ralph --help'." >&2
  exit 1
fi

if ! git rev-parse --git-dir > /dev/null 2>&1; then
  echo "ralph: not a git repo. Run /onboard or git init first — ralph relies on git history as memory between attempts." >&2
  exit 1
fi

mkdir -p .ralph/logs
RUN_LOG=".ralph/run.log"

remaining_items() {
  grep -cE '^[[:space:]]*-[[:space:]]\[[[:space:]]\]' "$PLAN" 2>/dev/null || true
}
next_item_text() {
  grep -m 1 -E '^[[:space:]]*-[[:space:]]\[[[:space:]]\]' "$PLAN" | sed -E 's/^[[:space:]]*-[[:space:]]\[[[:space:]]\][[:space:]]*//'
}
next_item_line_number() {
  grep -n -m 1 -E '^[[:space:]]*-[[:space:]]\[[[:space:]]\]' "$PLAN" | cut -d: -f1
}

is_mvn_item() {  # $1 = item text (post checkbox-strip)
  [[ "$1" =~ ^\[mvn\][[:space:]] ]]
}

# Parses a [mvn] item's text into MVN_DESC, MVN_GOAL, MVN_PROPS[], MVN_POM,
# MVN_THEN. Field values must not contain spaces (same constraint Maven's
# own -D flag has) except `then=`, which is required to be the LAST field
# and consumes everything to end of line, unsplit.
mvn_item_fields() {
  local body rest token
  body="${1#\[mvn\] }"
  MVN_DESC="${body%% ::*}"
  rest="${body#*:: }"
  MVN_GOAL=""
  MVN_PROPS=()
  MVN_POM=""
  MVN_THEN=""
  while [[ "$rest" != then=* ]]; do
    if [[ -z "$rest" ]]; then
      echo "ralph: malformed [mvn] item — missing required 'then=' field. See $PLAN: \"$1\"" >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    token="${rest%% *}"
    case "$token" in
      goal=*) MVN_GOAL="${token#goal=}" ;;
      prop=*) MVN_PROPS+=("${token#prop=}") ;;
      pom=*)  MVN_POM="${token#pom=}" ;;
      *) echo "ralph: malformed [mvn] item — unexpected field \"$token\" before 'then='. See $PLAN: \"$1\"" >&2; exit 1 ;;
    esac
    if [[ "$rest" == "$token" ]]; then
      rest=""
    else
      rest="${rest#"$token" }"
    fi
  done
  MVN_THEN="${rest#then=}"
  if [[ -z "$MVN_GOAL" ]]; then
    echo "ralph: malformed [mvn] item — missing required 'goal=' field. See $PLAN: \"$1\"" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  if [[ -z "$MVN_THEN" ]]; then
    echo "ralph: malformed [mvn] item — 'then=' field is empty. See $PLAN: \"$1\"" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
}

MODEL_FLAG=()
[[ -n "$MODEL" ]] && MODEL_FLAG=(--model "$MODEL")

echo "ralph: starting. $(remaining_items) unchecked item(s) in $PLAN." | tee -a "$RUN_LOG"

ITEMS_DONE=0
while true; do
  REMAINING=$(remaining_items)
  if [[ "$REMAINING" -eq 0 ]]; then
    echo "ralph: no unchecked items remain in $PLAN. Done." | tee -a "$RUN_LOG"
    exit 0
  fi
  if [[ "$ITEMS_LIMIT" -gt 0 && "$ITEMS_DONE" -ge "$ITEMS_LIMIT" ]]; then
    echo "ralph: completed $ITEMS_DONE item(s) as requested (--items $ITEMS_LIMIT). $REMAINING item(s) still remain — re-invoke to continue." | tee -a "$RUN_LOG"
    exit 0
  fi

  ITEM_TEXT=$(next_item_text)
  echo "ralph: next item — \"$ITEM_TEXT\"" | tee -a "$RUN_LOG"

  IS_MVN=false
  if is_mvn_item "$ITEM_TEXT"; then
    IS_MVN=true
    mvn_item_fields "$ITEM_TEXT"
    echo "ralph: [mvn] item — running directly via mvn, no claude -p call, no --review-gate: $MVN_DESC" | tee -a "$RUN_LOG"
  else
    case "$REVIEW_GATE" in
      full)
        REVIEW_STEP="4. Tests passing is necessary but not sufficient. Run \`/code-review high\` on your changes. If any finding survives its verification pass, fix it, re-run tests, and re-review, all within this same attempt if you can. Only once review comes back with no surviving findings do you move to step 5."
        ;;
      light)
        REVIEW_STEP="4. Tests passing is necessary but not sufficient. Run \`/code-review medium\` on your changes. If any finding survives its verification pass, fix it, re-run tests, and re-review, all within this same attempt if you can. Only once review comes back with no surviving findings do you move to step 5."
        ;;
      none)
        REVIEW_STEP="4. (No review gate configured — tests passing alone is sufficient. Move to step 5.)"
        ;;
    esac
  fi

  ITEM_SUCCEEDED=false
  for ((attempt=1; attempt<=MAX_ATTEMPTS; attempt++)); do
    LOG_FILE=".ralph/logs/item-$(printf '%03d' $((ITEMS_DONE+1)))-attempt-$attempt.log"

    if [[ "$IS_MVN" == "true" ]]; then
      MVN_CMD=(mvn)
      [[ -n "$MVN_POM" ]] && MVN_CMD+=(-f "$MVN_POM")
      MVN_CMD+=("$MVN_GOAL")
      for p in "${MVN_PROPS[@]:-}"; do
        [[ -n "$p" ]] && MVN_CMD+=("-D$p")
      done

      {
        echo "+ ${MVN_CMD[*]}"
        echo "+ $MVN_THEN"
      } > "$LOG_FILE"

      if "${MVN_CMD[@]}" >> "$LOG_FILE" 2>&1 && bash -c "$MVN_THEN" >> "$LOG_FILE" 2>&1; then
        LINE_NUM=$(next_item_line_number)
        sed -i "${LINE_NUM}s/^\([[:space:]]*-[[:space:]]\)\[ \]/\1[x]/" "$PLAN"
        git add -A
        git commit -q -m "ralph: [mvn] $MVN_DESC (no LLM turn)"
        echo "ralph: item done on attempt $attempt/$MAX_ATTEMPTS (mvn, no LLM) — $(git log -1 --format='%h %s')" | tee -a "$RUN_LOG"
        ITEM_SUCCEEDED=true
        ITEMS_DONE=$((ITEMS_DONE + 1))
        break
      else
        echo "ralph: attempt $attempt/$MAX_ATTEMPTS: [mvn] item failed (mvn or the verify command exited non-zero). Working tree left as-is for inspection. See $LOG_FILE." | tee -a "$RUN_LOG"
      fi
    else
      BEFORE_SHA=$(git rev-parse HEAD)

      PROMPT="You are one fresh-context attempt at a single task from $PLAN. You have NO memory of previous attempts — everything you need is in this repository's current state.

TASK: $ITEM_TEXT

1. Read \`git log --oneline -20\` — if a prior attempt on this exact task left partial work or a note, pick up from there instead of starting over blind.
2. Implement (or continue/fix) this task completely, including tests.
3. Run the test suite. If it doesn't pass, that's your only job this attempt — get it passing, or if you can't, skip to step 5.
$REVIEW_STEP
5. If everything in steps 3-4 that applies is clean: check off this item in $PLAN (change its '- [ ] $ITEM_TEXT' to '- [x] $ITEM_TEXT'), and commit everything with a specific message. This repo may also have a SPEC.md — the Design Reference this task's item may point to. Treat SPEC.md as strictly read-only: never edit it, and never check anything off in it. All checklist state belongs only in $PLAN.
6. If it does NOT pass after your best effort this attempt: commit whatever partial progress is safe to commit, with a clear message describing exactly what you tried, what's still failing (test failures or unresolved review findings), so the next attempt isn't starting from zero. Do NOT check off the item.

Work on ONLY this task. Do not start any other item, do not refactor unrelated code."

      claude -p "$PROMPT" --permission-mode auto "${MODEL_FLAG[@]}" > "$LOG_FILE" 2>&1 || true

      AFTER_SHA=$(git rev-parse HEAD)
      NEW_REMAINING=$(remaining_items)

      if [[ "$NEW_REMAINING" -lt "$REMAINING" ]]; then
        echo "ralph: item done on attempt $attempt/$MAX_ATTEMPTS — $(git log -1 --format='%h %s')" | tee -a "$RUN_LOG"
        ITEM_SUCCEEDED=true
        ITEMS_DONE=$((ITEMS_DONE + 1))
        break
      elif [[ "$BEFORE_SHA" == "$AFTER_SHA" ]]; then
        echo "ralph: attempt $attempt/$MAX_ATTEMPTS made no commit at all. See $LOG_FILE." | tee -a "$RUN_LOG"
      else
        echo "ralph: attempt $attempt/$MAX_ATTEMPTS committed partial progress, item not yet passing. See $LOG_FILE." | tee -a "$RUN_LOG"
      fi
    fi
  done

  if [[ "$ITEM_SUCCEEDED" != "true" ]]; then
    echo "ralph: exhausted $MAX_ATTEMPTS attempts on \"$ITEM_TEXT\" without success — stopping for human review. Check $PLAN's notes (and SPEC.md's Design Reference for the item's design constraints) and the .ralph/logs/item-$(printf '%03d' $((ITEMS_DONE+1)))-attempt-*.log files." | tee -a "$RUN_LOG"
    exit 2
  fi
done
